{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"hypothesis"},"hash":"ed2ebf1c09a60311263f70878a0b8056ad7627ed11a72662751a41ef4f8f703a","kind":"stage","prev":"e2321d31a33b380dfcbc813479f5ddc03860f516c0616e83ca0776b03af30515","seq":15,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.984+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["generalizability","alternative methods","dataset diversity","distance metrics"],"description":"The evidence primarily focuses on two specific datasets—stroke and Pima Indians Diabetes—both of which are used to compare z-score standardization and min-max normalization in the context of k-nearest-neighbor classification. While these studies support the idea that feature scaling improves KNN accuracy, the generalizability of these findings to a broader range of tabular datasets with varying feature distributions, missing values, or class imbalances remains untested. Evidence E1 and E2 do not evaluate other scaling methods (e.g., robust scaling, decimal scaling) or alternative distance metrics (e.g., Manhattan vs. Euclidean), which could influence performance. Thus, the claim that standardization improves accuracy on tabular data with different scales is tested only narrowly.","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"id":"G1","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"0caa03c7bbd38d2000487c9c9e841cad3379949162d5bda226e43cd9dbcea3fa","kind":"artefact","prev":"ed2ebf1c09a60311263f70878a0b8056ad7627ed11a72662751a41ef4f8f703a","seq":16,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.984+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["preprocessing pipeline","feature selection","outlier handling","categorical encoding"],"description":"The impact of standardization on accuracy is evaluated only in comparison to min-max normalization, not in isolation or in relation to other preprocessing steps such as feature selection, outlier handling, or encoding categorical variables. Therefore, the evidence does not address whether standardization is superior, inferior, or equivalent in performance when combined with other preprocessing strategies. This leaves open the question of whether standardization's benefits are context-dependent beyond just scale normalization.","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"id":"G2","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"87c582a991bc76c3cc3010604375aaada9dcf9fcf9f4875dae85ca8ba52fef48","kind":"artefact","prev":"0caa03c7bbd38d2000487c9c9e841cad3379949162d5bda226e43cd9dbcea3fa","seq":17,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"hypothesis"},"hash":"2052de84b7058208d5e69abaa7fe68b8eaf460031f1342fe1f16bd6f352a2c01","kind":"stage","prev":"87c582a991bc76c3cc3010604375aaada9dcf9fcf9f4875dae85ca8ba52fef48","seq":18,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"classification accuracy","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"expected_direction":"increase","gap_ids":["G1"],"id":"H1","independent_variable":"scaling method (z-score vs. min-max)","rationale":"This hypothesis directly addresses the narrow scope of existing evidence by testing the relative performance of standardization versus normalization across a broader class of tabular datasets. It also incorporates the specific distance metric (Euclidean), which is not evaluated in the current evidence, thus addressing the gap in distance metric diversity. The claim that z-score standardization yields higher accuracy than min-max normalization, despite E1 and E2 suggesting otherwise, is falsifiable by testing on diverse public datasets (e.g., adult income, boston housing, or credit approval) with non-normal distributions and varying feature scales.","statement":"For tabular datasets with mixed feature scales and no missing values, z-score standardization will achieve higher classification accuracy than min-max normalization when using Euclidean distance in k-nearest-neighbor classification."},"hash":"332acee951244808e6d821c70c25b52cfe2908a9985feff97a494a2d3c238f1d","kind":"artefact","prev":"2052de84b7058208d5e69abaa7fe68b8eaf460031f1342fe1f16bd6f352a2c01","seq":19,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"classification accuracy","evidence_ids":["E1","E2"],"expected_direction":"increase","gap_ids":["G2"],"id":"H2","independent_variable":"preprocessing pipeline (with vs. without outlier handling)","rationale":"This hypothesis targets the gap in preprocessing pipeline integration. It posits that standardization alone may not capture the full benefit of preprocessing, and that combining scaling with outlier handling could enhance performance—particularly in datasets where extreme values distort neighborhood definitions in KNN. This directly extends beyond the isolated scaling comparisons in E1 and E2 to test context-dependency, as per G2.","statement":"When feature scaling is combined with outlier detection and removal, k-nearest-neighbor classification accuracy improves more than when scaling is applied in isolation, particularly in datasets with skewed or heavy-tailed feature distributions."},"hash":"249773f11589b7a0b0bd5c3ec730024374914e97ee01e9e4c0cad06d73f4c44b","kind":"artefact","prev":"332acee951244808e6d821c70c25b52cfe2908a9985feff97a494a2d3c238f1d","seq":20,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
