{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"experiment"},"hash":"7d6002b8306923e8e3db97af50feb5395eb537ef89e1132003f801b2582468b3","kind":"stage","prev":"ec38a7e5b578df5d85c45f042ce2ed76f4cf5d3eb4303653d370711a37bd090e","seq":22,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer","wine_binary","digits_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H1","id":"D1","metrics":["accuracy"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on classification accuracy under different scaling methods when using Euclidean distance in k-nearest-neighbor (knn) classification. To isolate the effect of scaling (z-score vs. min-max), we use knn as the base model, as it relies on distance metrics and is sensitive to feature scaling. The control condition (knn) serves as a baseline for comparison. The treatment conditions include knn+standardize (representing z-score standardization) and other modifiers (platt, isotonic) to explore additional model adjustments, though the primary focus is on scaling. We exclude datasets with missing values and imbalanced classes (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10, digits_binary_imb10) to maintain the condition of 'no missing values' and avoid confounding by class imbalance. The chosen datasets (breast_cancer, wine_binary, digits_binary) are tabular, have mixed feature scales, and no missing values. We use 5-fold cross-validation to ensure robustness and apply paired Wilcoxon test to compare accuracy between the control and treatment conditions, ensuring statistical validity. Seeds are specified to allow reproducibility. This design directly tests the hypothesis by varying scaling methods under Euclidean distance, with the dependent variable (accuracy) measured across diverse, real-world tabular datasets.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize","knn+platt","knn+isotonic"]},"hash":"b6f59693d40903c79b141569a6feda76a09845e1a64a4425c31ba9434c9a08f0","kind":"artefact","prev":"7d6002b8306923e8e3db97af50feb5395eb537ef89e1132003f801b2582468b3","seq":23,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.985+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn+standardize","datasets":["breast_cancer","digits_binary","wine_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"D2","metrics":["accuracy"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on whether combining feature scaling with outlier detection and removal improves k-nearest-neighbor classification accuracy—particularly in datasets with skewed or heavy-tailed distributions. To test this, we select three datasets: breast_cancer (real-world medical data with potential outliers), digits_binary (high-dimensional with possible heavy-tailed features), and wine_binary (moderately sized with plausible distributional skew). These are chosen for their relevance to real-world data and varying feature distributions. The control condition (knn+standardize) isolates the effect of scaling, while the treatment (knn+standardize+outlier_removal) introduces the proposed intervention. We use accuracy as the primary metric, as specified in the hypothesis. A paired Wilcoxon test is appropriate because it assesses differences in accuracy between the control and treatment under the same model and data structure, with repeated measures from cross-validation. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness and stability in performance estimation. Three random seeds ensure reproducibility and help verify consistency across runs. The exclusion of imbalanced datasets (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10, digits_binary_imb10) is intentional—since the hypothesis emphasizes distributional skew and heavy tails, not class imbalance, and the core effect is on feature space geometry, not class balance. Imbalanced datasets may introduce confounding factors related to minority class performance, which are outside the scope of the current hypothesis.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize+outlier_removal"]},"hash":"be287fc8c8c5deab6bbfaa5fe7aac7d87faba3121cbeef5ebc4c63277cc83e89","kind":"artefact","prev":"b6f59693d40903c79b141569a6feda76a09845e1a64a4425c31ba9434c9a08f0","seq":24,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.986+00:00"}
